---
// Goal: show a newcomer in one minute that ten functions put a decision
// model in their code, what they gain over calling the model directly,
// that it fits their setup, and what to paste first.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { CODE_FUNCTIONS, FUNCTIONS, COUNTS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { run } from '../data/samples.mjs';
import { REPO } from '../data/repo.mjs';

const decide = FUNCTIONS.find((f) => f.name === 'decide');
const refund = { ...run('functions/decide', '0-refund'), see: decide.see['0-refund'] };
const batching = `${REPO}/blob/main/sdlc/planning/adr/0055-decide-filter-and-rank-batches-send-each-record-once.md`;
const findRecord = `${REPO}/blob/main/specification/find.md#what-the-live-checks-measured`;
---
<Base title="ThinkThen" description="Ten functions that put a decision model in your code. Branch on yes, no or not sure, pay once for each answer, and test with no key.">
  <h1>Code that knows what you mean.</h1>
  <p class="lede">ThinkThen gives you ten functions that put a decision model in your code. Ask a question in plain words and get back an answer your code can branch on. You pay for fewer requests, you test with no key, and you tune with no new calls.</p>

  <Terminal run={refund} caption={refund.see} />

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Decisions your code can branch on.</b> <code>decide</code> exits 0 for yes, 1 for no and 3 for not sure, and a failure never looks like "no". <a href="/learn/answers/#exit-codes">How answers work</a></li>
    <li><b>Pay once.</b> 306 song titles went out in one request: <a href={batching}>11,468 input tokens instead of 88,933</a> one at a time. Each question keeps its own saved answer, however records group. <a href="/start/pay-once/">Pay once</a></li>
    <li><b>Test offline.</b> A test replays saved answers with no key and no network. <a href="/start/replay/">Replay in tests</a></li>
    <li><b>Tune for free.</b> Move the cut on saved answers with zero new calls. <code>runs audit</code> picks the cut from your own labels. <a href="/start/tune/">Tune for free</a></li>
    <li><b>See the cost before you send.</b> <code>--plan</code> shows the requests and the expected tokens, and sends nothing. <a href="/start/plan/">See before you send</a></li>
    <li><b>Structure from decisions.</b> <a href="/functions/recognize/"><code>recognize</code></a> pulls names out of text. <a href="/functions/find/"><code>find</code></a> picks the one line that answers, or says nothing fits. On twenty test documents it sent <a href={findRecord}>20 requests where ranking every line sent 239</a>. <a href="/functions/relate/"><code>relate</code></a> links the named things.</li>
    <li><b>Your data and keys stay put.</b> A field pointer sends one field of each record. Your key never shows in a plan, a result, a recording or an error. ThinkThen never acts on what it reads. <a href="/start/your-data/">Your data stays yours</a></li>
    <li><b>One engine everywhere.</b> The same engine runs in every language and database, on any model you can swap and check. It adds about 2 ms to a call. <a href="/start/speed/">Speed</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <p class="counts">{COUNTS.functions}, <a href="/languages/bash/">{COUNTS.cli}</a>, <a href="/languages/">{COUNTS.bindings}</a>.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Function</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <tr><td><a href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}><code>{fn.name}</code></a></td><td>{fn.line}</td></tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question as a file</a></p>

  <h2>Fits your setup</h2>
  <p>Start in <a href="/languages/bash/">Bash</a>, then call the same functions from <a href="/languages/python/">Python</a>, <a href="/languages/typescript/">TypeScript</a> and <a href="/languages/">every other language</a>. Ask them in SQL inside <a href="/install/duckdb/">DuckDB</a>, <a href="/install/sqlite/">SQLite</a> and <a href="/install/postgresql/">PostgreSQL</a>. Point them at TypeSafe's Jev, another provider or a model on your own machine with <a href="/install/backends/">one backend option</a>.</p>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/start/">Install and get a first answer</a> · <a href="/start/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> · <a href="/recipes/">Recipes</a> · <a href="/learn/beatles-bench/">Beatles Bench</a></p>
</Base>
